---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About">
  <h1>About this project</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Middle East Explorer helps students, from high school upward, learn about the
    economics and geopolitics of the Middle East and how the region interacts
    with major powers.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <p>
      Click a country on the globe to see its energy exposure. Middle East countries
      have full profile pages with indicators, what's hitting them now, and an escape
      plan. External powers (the United States, China, India, Russia, the United
      Kingdom, South Korea, Japan and the European Union, which is treated as one
      bloc) are the buyers and counterparts: click one to see its role, with no page
      of its own. Click a chokepoint or buyer to see how a shock reaches other
      countries. You can also <a href="/browse">browse all pages</a> as a simple list.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Sources policy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Figures and facts are entered by hand and cited to their sources.</li>
      <li>Every country and chain page lists its sources and its last-updated date.</li>
      <li>
        A field that hasn't been filled in yet says <span class="todo">TODO: fill in</span>.
        Nothing is estimated to fill a gap.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Map boundaries</h2>
    <p>
      {/* Keep links on the same line as the words around them, or the spaces get lost. */}
      Country shapes come from <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Natural Earth</a> and are shown unchanged.
      Their use here doesn't represent a position on any disputed border or territory.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
